<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>thermal-expansion</title></head><body><h1>thermal-expansion</h1><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li>Linear Expansion and Its Measurement</li><li>Linear Expansion</li><li>Cubical and Superficial Expansion</li><li>Cubical Expansion, Superficial Expansion and Their Relation with Linear Expansion</li><li>Cubical and Superficial Expansion</li><li>Liquid Expansion — Absolute and Apparent</li><li>Liquid Expansion</li><li>Dulong and Petit Method for Liquid Expansivity</li><li>Dulong and Petit Method of Determining Expansivity of Liquid</li><li>Dulong and Petit Method</li></ul><h2>Linear Expansion and Its Measurement</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Linear expansion definition:** When a solid is heated, its length increases. The fractional increase in length per unit rise in temperature is called the coefficient of linear expansion, $\alpha$.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Formula:** $\Delta L = L_0 \alpha \Delta T$, where $L_0$ is the original length, $\alpha$ is the coefficient of linear expansion (unit: K⁻¹ or °C⁻¹), and $\Delta T$ is the temperature change.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**New length:** $L = L_0(1 + \alpha\Delta T)$. For small $\alpha\Delta T$, this is an excellent approximation. For large temperature ranges, $\alpha$ may vary and must be treated as a function of temperature.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Physical origin:** Heating increases the amplitude of atomic vibrations in the lattice. Because the interatomic potential is asymmetric (steeper on the compression side), the average separation increases with temperature, causing expansion.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Coefficient of linear expansion for common materials:** Steel: $\alpha \approx 12 \times 10^{-6}$ K⁻¹. Aluminum: $\alpha \approx 24 \times 10^{-6}$ K⁻¹. Copper: $\alpha \approx 17 \times 10^{-6}$ K⁻¹. Invar (Fe-Ni alloy): $\alpha \approx 1.2 \times 10^{-6}$ K⁻¹. Quartz: $\alpha \approx 0.5 \times 10^{-6}$ K⁻¹.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Why metals expand more:** Metals with weaker interatomic bonds (larger bond length, lower melting point) tend to have larger $\alpha$. Lead ($\alpha \approx 29 \times 10^{-6}$) expands more than steel because its atomic bonds are weaker.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Bimetallic strip:** Two metals with different $\alpha$ bonded together bend when heated — the higher-$\alpha$ metal expands more, forcing the strip to curve. Used in thermostats, circuit breakers, and temperature indicators.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Railway tracks — expansion gaps:** Steel rails expand by $\Delta L = L_0 \alpha \Delta T$. For a 12 m rail and $\Delta T = 40°C$: $\Delta L = 12 \times 12 \times 10^{-6} \times 40 = 5.76$ mm. Gaps are left between rails to accommodate this expansion, otherwise buckling occurs.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Measuring $\alpha$ — Searle's apparatus:** A metal rod is heated uniformly. The expansion is measured by a micrometer screw gauge. $\alpha = \frac{\Delta L}{L_0 \Delta T}$. The apparatus minimizes errors from thermal expansion of the frame.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>**Measuring $\alpha$ — Hoke's apparatus:** Uses a glass tube filled with liquid. As the solid rod inside expands, it displaces liquid. The volume of displaced liquid gives $\Delta L$ indirectly. This is an elegant optical-mechanical method.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>**NEB exam focus:** State the formula $\Delta L = L_0 \alpha \Delta T$. Explain the bimetallic strip principle. Describe Searle's apparatus for measuring $\alpha$. Solve numerical problems involving expansion of rods, bridges, and railways.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>**Effect on precision instruments:** Clock pendulums expand with temperature, increasing their period and causing the clock to run slow. $T = 2\pi\sqrt{L/g}$, so $\Delta T/T = \frac{1}{2}\alpha\Delta T$. For steel ($\alpha = 12 \times 10^{-6}$), a 10°C rise causes the clock to lose about 5 seconds per day.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>**Composite rod problem:** Two rods of different materials joined end-to-end: total expansion $\Delta L = L_{01}\alpha_1\Delta T + L_{02}\alpha_2\Delta T$. The junction shifts by the expansion of the first rod.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>**Thermal stress:** If a rod is constrained from expanding, thermal stress develops: $\sigma = Y\alpha\Delta T$, where $Y$ is Young's modulus. For steel ($Y = 200$ GPa, $\alpha = 12 \times 10^{-6}$), a 50°C constraint gives $\sigma = 120$ MPa — significant structural stress.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>**Practical consequence:** Bridges have expansion joints. Glass bottles crack when hot water is poured in because the inner surface expands faster than the outer. Telephone wires sag more in summer than in winter.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ $\Delta L = L_0 \alpha \Delta T$ works for any temperature range. ✅ It is valid only for moderate $\Delta T$ where $\alpha$ is approximately constant. For very large temperature ranges, $\alpha$ varies and the formula becomes approximate.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ All solids expand at the same rate. ✅ Different materials have very different $\alpha$ values. Invar ($\alpha \approx 1.2 \times 10^{-6}$) expands 24 times less than aluminum ($\alpha \approx 24 \times 10^{-6}$).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ The coefficient $\alpha$ has units of temperature. ✅ $\alpha$ has units of K⁻¹ (or °C⁻¹). It is a fractional change per degree, not an absolute change.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ A hole in a metal plate shrinks when heated. ✅ The hole EXPANDS — the material around it expands outward, making the hole larger. This is equivalent to a disk of the same material expanding.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Linear expansion applies to liquids. ✅ Liquids do not have a fixed shape, so linear expansion is meaningless for them. Only cubical (volume) expansion is defined for liquids.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ The expansion gap for railway tracks should be as large as possible. ✅ The gap must be just large enough to prevent buckling. Too large a gap causes excessive noise and wear at the joints.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Thermal stress depends only on $\alpha$ and $\Delta T$. ✅ Thermal stress is $\sigma = Y\alpha\Delta T$ — it also depends on Young's modulus $Y$. A stiffer material develops more stress for the same constraint.</td></tr></table><h2>Linear Expansion</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Linear Expansion:** This topic covers the fundamental concepts of linear expansion in the physics curriculum.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Understanding linear expansion is essential for solving problems and building higher-level mathematical skills.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Key definitions and theorems related to linear expansion should be memorized and practiced regularly.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Linear expansion definition:** When a solid is heated, its length increases. The fractional increase in length per unit rise in temperature is called the coefficient of linear expansion, $\alpha$.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Formula:** $\Delta L = L_0 \alpha \Delta T$, where $L_0$ is the original length, $\alpha$ is the coefficient of linear expansion (unit: K⁻¹ or °C⁻¹), and $\Delta T$ is the temperature change.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**New length:** $L = L_0(1 + \alpha\Delta T)$. For small $\alpha\Delta T$, this is an excellent approximation. For large temperature ranges, $\alpha$ may vary and must be treated as a function of temperature.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Physical origin:** Heating increases the amplitude of atomic vibrations in the lattice. Because the interatomic potential is asymmetric (steeper on the compression side), the average separation increases with temperature, causing expansion.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Coefficient of linear expansion for common materials:** Steel: $\alpha \approx 12 \times 10^{-6}$ K⁻¹. Aluminum: $\alpha \approx 24 \times 10^{-6}$ K⁻¹. Copper: $\alpha \approx 17 \times 10^{-6}$ K⁻¹. Invar (Fe-Ni alloy): $\alpha \approx 1.2 \times 10^{-6}$ K⁻¹. Quartz: $\alpha \approx 0.5 \times 10^{-6}$ K⁻¹.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Why metals expand more:** Metals with weaker interatomic bonds (larger bond length, lower melting point) tend to have larger $\alpha$. Lead ($\alpha \approx 29 \times 10^{-6}$) expands more than steel because its atomic bonds are weaker.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>**Bimetallic strip:** Two metals with different $\alpha$ bonded together bend when heated — the higher-$\alpha$ metal expands more, forcing the strip to curve. Used in thermostats, circuit breakers, and temperature indicators.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>**Railway tracks — expansion gaps:** Steel rails expand by $\Delta L = L_0 \alpha \Delta T$. For a 12 m rail and $\Delta T = 40°C$: $\Delta L = 12 \times 12 \times 10^{-6} \times 40 = 5.76$ mm. Gaps are left between rails to accommodate this expansion, otherwise buckling occurs.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>**Measuring $\alpha$ — Searle's apparatus:** A metal rod is heated uniformly. The expansion is measured by a micrometer screw gauge. $\alpha = \frac{\Delta L}{L_0 \Delta T}$. The apparatus minimizes errors from thermal expansion of the frame.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>**Measuring $\alpha$ — Hoke's apparatus:** Uses a glass tube filled with liquid. As the solid rod inside expands, it displaces liquid. The volume of displaced liquid gives $\Delta L$ indirectly. This is an elegant optical-mechanical method.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>**NEB exam focus:** State the formula $\Delta L = L_0 \alpha \Delta T$. Explain the bimetallic strip principle. Describe Searle's apparatus for measuring $\alpha$. Solve numerical problems involving expansion of rods, bridges, and railways.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>**Effect on precision instruments:** Clock pendulums expand with temperature, increasing their period and causing the clock to run slow. $T = 2\pi\sqrt{L/g}$, so $\Delta T/T = \frac{1}{2}\alpha\Delta T$. For steel ($\alpha = 12 \times 10^{-6}$), a 10°C rise causes the clock to lose about 5 seconds per day.</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>**Composite rod problem:** Two rods of different materials joined end-to-end: total expansion $\Delta L = L_{01}\alpha_1\Delta T + L_{02}\alpha_2\Delta T$. The junction shifts by the expansion of the first rod.</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>**Thermal stress:** If a rod is constrained from expanding, thermal stress develops: $\sigma = Y\alpha\Delta T$, where $Y$ is Young's modulus. For steel ($Y = 200$ GPa, $\alpha = 12 \times 10^{-6}$), a 50°C constraint gives $\sigma = 120$ MPa — significant structural stress.</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>**Practical consequence:** Bridges have expansion joints. Glass bottles crack when hot water is poured in because the inner surface expands faster than the outer. Telephone wires sag more in summer than in winter.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Key Point 2: Essential theorem or principle related to Linear Expansion</td></tr><tr><td>Key Point 3: Important condition or constraint for Linear Expansion</td></tr><tr><td>Key Point 4: Common applications of Linear Expansion</td></tr><tr><td>Key Point 5: Connection to other topics in Science</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Carefully distinguish between similar-looking concepts in linear expansion.</td></tr><tr><td>Pay attention to the conditions under which formulas and theorems apply.</td></tr><tr><td>Practice with multiple examples to solidify your understanding.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ $\Delta L = L_0 \alpha \Delta T$ works for any temperature range. ✅ It is valid only for moderate $\Delta T$ where $\alpha$ is approximately constant. For very large temperature ranges, $\alpha$ varies and the formula becomes approximate.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ All solids expand at the same rate. ✅ Different materials have very different $\alpha$ values. Invar ($\alpha \approx 1.2 \times 10^{-6}$) expands 24 times less than aluminum ($\alpha \approx 24 \times 10^{-6}$).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ The coefficient $\alpha$ has units of temperature. ✅ $\alpha$ has units of K⁻¹ (or °C⁻¹). It is a fractional change per degree, not an absolute change.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ A hole in a metal plate shrinks when heated. ✅ The hole EXPANDS — the material around it expands outward, making the hole larger. This is equivalent to a disk of the same material expanding.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Linear expansion applies to liquids. ✅ Liquids do not have a fixed shape, so linear expansion is meaningless for them. Only cubical (volume) expansion is defined for liquids.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ The expansion gap for railway tracks should be as large as possible. ✅ The gap must be just large enough to prevent buckling. Too large a gap causes excessive noise and wear at the joints.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Thermal stress depends only on $\alpha$ and $\Delta T$. ✅ Thermal stress is $\sigma = Y\alpha\Delta T$ — it also depends on Young's modulus $Y$. A stiffer material develops more stress for the same constraint.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Linear Expansion is a fundamental concept in Science that covers the essential principles, formulas, and applications. Understanding this topic is crucial for solving related problems and building foundation for advanced topics.</p><hr><h2>Cubical and Superficial Expansion</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Superficial (area) expansion:** When a solid sheet is heated, its area increases. $\Delta A = A_0 \beta \Delta T$, where $\beta$ is the coefficient of superficial expansion.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Cubical (volume) expansion:** When a solid is heated, its volume increases. $\Delta V = V_0 \gamma \Delta T$, where $\gamma$ is the coefficient of cubical expansion.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Relation for isotropic solids:** $\alpha : \beta : \gamma = 1 : 2 : 3$. That is, $\beta = 2\alpha$ and $\gamma = 3\alpha$. This holds for isotropic materials (same properties in all directions).</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Derivation of $\beta = 2\alpha$:** For a square of side $L$: $A = L^2 = L_0^2(1 + \alpha\Delta T)^2 \approx L_0^2(1 + 2\alpha\Delta T)$ for small $\alpha\Delta T$. Therefore $\beta = 2\alpha$.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Derivation of $\gamma = 3\alpha$:** For a cube of side $L$: $V = L^3 = L_0^3(1 + \alpha\Delta T)^3 \approx L_0^3(1 + 3\alpha\Delta T)$ for small $\alpha\Delta T$. Therefore $\gamma = 3\alpha$.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Why the ratios hold:** Each dimension (length, breadth, height) expands independently by the factor $(1 + \alpha\Delta T)$. Area involves two dimensions → factor $(1 + \alpha\Delta T)^2 \approx 1 + 2\alpha\Delta T$. Volume involves three → $1 + 3\alpha\Delta T$.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Anisotropic crystals:** In crystals like graphite or calcite, expansion differs along different axes. The relation $\gamma = \alpha_1 + \alpha_2 + \alpha_3$ still holds, where $\alpha_i$ are the linear coefficients along the three principal axes.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Holes and cavities:** A hole in a metal plate expands AS IF it were filled with the same material. The cavity behaves like a solid piece — its volume increases with $\gamma$. This is because the material around the hole expands outward.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Liquids have no $\alpha$ or $\beta$:** Liquids cannot sustain shear stress, so they have no fixed shape. Only cubical (volume) expansion is defined for liquids. $\gamma_{liquid}$ is typically much larger than $\gamma_{solid}$.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>**Glass vessels and liquids:** When a liquid in a glass vessel is heated, BOTH expand. The liquid level may first fall (glass expands first) then rise (liquid expands more). The apparent expansion measures only the net effect.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>**NEB exam focus:** Derive $\beta = 2\alpha$ and $\gamma = 3\alpha$. Solve problems involving expansion of area and volume. Distinguish between absolute and apparent expansion of liquids.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>**Numerical example — area expansion:** An aluminum plate has area 1.000 m² at 20°C. At 100°C: $\Delta A = 1.000 \times 2 \times 24 \times 10^{-6} \times 80 = 3.84 \times 10^{-3}$ m². New area = 1.00384 m².</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>**Numerical example — volume expansion:** A glass flask of 1000 cm³ is filled with mercury at 20°C. Both are heated to 100°C. $\gamma_{glass} = 3\alpha_{glass} = 3 \times 9 \times 10^{-6} = 27 \times 10^{-6}$. $\gamma_{Hg} = 182 \times 10^{-6}$. Overflow = $1000 \times (182 - 27) \times 10^{-6} \times 80 = 12.4$ cm³.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>**Practical applications:** (1) Mercury thermometers rely on the large $\gamma$ of mercury vs. glass. (2) Expansion joints in pipelines accommodate volume changes. (3) Tight metal lids loosen when heated (the lid expands more than the jar).</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>**Dimensional analysis:** $[\alpha] = [\beta] = [\gamma] = [\Theta^{-1}] = [K^{-1}]$. All three coefficients have the same dimensions — they differ only in magnitude by factors of 2 and 3 for isotropic solids.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Key Point 2: Essential theorem or principle related to Cubical expansion, superficial expansion and their relation with linear expansion</td></tr><tr><td>Key Point 3: Important condition or constraint for Cubical expansion, superficial expansion and their relation with linear expansion</td></tr><tr><td>Key Point 4: Common applications of Cubical expansion, superficial expansion and their relation with linear expansion</td></tr><tr><td>Key Point 5: Connection to other topics in Science</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ The relation $\gamma = 3\alpha$ holds for all materials including liquids. ✅ It holds only for ISOTROPIC SOLIDS. Liquids only have $\gamma$ — they have no $\alpha$ or $\beta$ because they have no fixed shape.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ A hole in a metal plate gets smaller when heated. ✅ The hole EXPANDS. The material around it moves outward equally in all directions, so the hole gets larger.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ $\alpha$, $\beta$, and $\gamma$ have different units. ✅ All three have the same units: K⁻¹ or °C⁻¹. They differ only in numerical value for isotropic solids ($\beta = 2\alpha$, $\gamma = 3\alpha$).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ The expansion ratios $\beta = 2\alpha$ and $\gamma = 3\alpha$ are exact for all temperature ranges. ✅ They are approximations valid when $\alpha\Delta T \ll 1$. For large $\Delta T$, higher-order terms become significant.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Anisotropic crystals have $\gamma = 3\alpha$. ✅ For anisotropic crystals, $\gamma = \alpha_1 + \alpha_2 + \alpha_3$, where the three $\alpha_i$ may be different along different crystal axes.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Only solids expand when heated — liquids and gases do not. ✅ Liquids and gases expand significantly more than solids. Mercury in a thermometer rises because liquid expansion is much larger than glass expansion.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ The volume of a cavity inside a solid does not change when heated. ✅ The cavity expands exactly as if it were filled with the solid material. The surrounding material expands outward, enlarging the cavity.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Cubical expansion, superficial expansion and their relation with linear expansion is a fundamental concept in Science that covers the essential principles, formulas, and applications. Understanding this topic is crucial for solving related problems and building foundation for advanced topics.</p><hr><h2>Cubical Expansion, Superficial Expansion and Their Relation with Linear Expansion</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Linear Expansion: When a solid is heated, its length increases: ΔL = αL₀ΔT where α is coefficient of linear expansion (K⁻¹), L₀ original length, ΔT temperature rise.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Superficial (Area) Expansion: Area increases: ΔA = βA₀ΔT where β ≈ 2α is coefficient of superficial expansion. For isotropic solid, β = 2α.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Cubical (Volume) Expansion: Volume increases: ΔV = γV₀ΔT where γ ≈ 3α is coefficient of cubical expansion. For isotropic solid, γ = 3α.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Relations: α : β : γ = 1 : 2 : 3 for isotropic solids. β = 2α, γ = 3α.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Isotropic vs Anisotropic: Relations β = 2α, γ = 3α hold for isotropic materials. For anisotropic (crystals), expansion differs along different axes.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Liquid Expansion: Liquids have only cubical expansion (no fixed shape). Apparent expansion differs from real expansion due to container expansion.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Real vs Apparent Expansion of Liquids: γ_real = γ_apparent + γ_container. The container expands too, so apparent rise is less than real expansion.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Anomalous Expansion of Water: Water contracts from 0°C to 4°C (maximum density at 4°C), then expands above 4°C. Negative expansion coefficient below 4°C.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Linear: ΔL = αL₀ΔT; coefficient α (K⁻¹).</td></tr><tr><td>Superficial: ΔA = βA₀ΔT, β = 2α.</td></tr><tr><td>Cubical: ΔV = γV₀ΔT, γ = 3α.</td></tr><tr><td>Relation α : β : γ = 1 : 2 : 3 for isotropic solids.</td></tr><tr><td>Relations valid only for isotropic materials.</td></tr><tr><td>Liquids have only volume expansion; real = apparent + container.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Applying γ = 3α to anisotropic crystals: Only valid for isotropic materials.</td></tr><tr><td>Forgetting container expansion in liquid problems: Real expansion = apparent + container.</td></tr><tr><td>Confusing coefficient of linear expansion with fractional expansion: α = ΔL/(L₀ΔT).</td></tr><tr><td>Using Celsius vs Kelvin for ΔT: Only ΔT matters, same in °C or K.</td></tr><tr><td>Neglecting higher order terms: α²ΔT² is tiny (~10⁻¹⁰) and ignored.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Linear ΔL = αL₀ΔT, superficial ΔA = βA₀ΔT (β = 2α), cubical ΔV = γV₀ΔT (γ = 3α) for isotropic solids. α:β:γ = 1:2:3. Liquids have only volume expansion; real = apparent + container. Water shows anomalous expansion below 4°C.</p><hr><h2>Cubical and Superficial Expansion</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Superficial expansion.** A heated sheet grows in area as ΔA = A₀βt where β is the areal expansion coefficient; for isotropic solids β = 2α, α being the linear coefficient.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Cubical expansion.** A heated solid grows in volume as ΔV = V₀γt with γ = 3α — the cube relation follows because V = L³, so ΔV/V = 3ΔL/L.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Derivation.** From V = L³, differentiating gives dV/V = 3 dL/L; similarly dA/A = 2 dL/L — hence β : γ = 2 : 3 relative to α.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Holes and gaps.** A hole in a heated plate expands exactly as solid material would — the hole gets bigger, not smaller; railway gaps and bimetal strips exploit this.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Numerical habit.** Convert every temperature rise to Δt, pick the right coefficient (α, β or γ), then apply Δ = initial × coefficient × Δt with consistent units.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Carefully distinguish between similar-looking concepts in cubical and superficial expansion.</td></tr><tr><td>Pay attention to the conditions under which formulas and theorems apply.</td></tr><tr><td>Practice with multiple examples to solidify your understanding.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Cubical and Superficial Expansion is a fundamental concept in Science that covers the essential principles, formulas, and applications. Understanding this topic is crucial for solving related problems and building foundation for advanced topics.</p><hr><h2>Liquid Expansion — Absolute and Apparent</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Why liquids need special treatment:** Unlike solids, liquids cannot maintain a fixed shape and must be contained. When heated, BOTH the liquid and the container expand, making direct measurement of liquid expansion impossible without correction.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Absolute (real) expansion:** The actual increase in volume of the liquid itself, independent of the container. $\Delta V_{abs} = V_0 \gamma_{liq} \Delta T$, where $\gamma_{liq}$ is the coefficient of cubical expansion of the liquid.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Apparent expansion:** The observed increase in volume of the liquid as read from the container's scale. Since the container also expands, the scale marks move apart, making the liquid level appear to rise LESS than it actually does.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Formula:** $\gamma_{app} = \gamma_{liq} - \gamma_{container}$. Therefore $\gamma_{liq} = \gamma_{app} + \gamma_{container}$. The absolute expansion equals the apparent expansion plus the container's expansion.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Physical picture:** Imagine a flask marked with graduated lines. When heated, the glass expands and the marks move farther apart. The liquid also expands and rises. The net rise on the (now-spreading) scale is the apparent expansion.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Mercury thermometer principle:** Mercury has $\gamma \approx 182 \times 10^{-6}$ K⁻¹ while glass has $\gamma \approx 27 \times 10^{-6}$ K⁻¹. The apparent expansion coefficient is $\gamma_{app} = 155 \times 10^{-6}$ K⁻¹, large enough to give readable scale divisions.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Anomalous expansion of water:** Water CONTRACTS from 0°C to 4°C and expands above 4°C. Its density is maximum at 4°C ($\rho = 1.0000$ g/cm³). Below 4°C, $\gamma$ is negative — water behaves opposite to normal liquids.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Consequence for lakes:** In winter, surface water cools to 4°C and sinks (maximum density). Water below 4°C is less dense and stays at the bottom. Ice forms at 0°C on the surface, insulating the water below and allowing aquatic life to survive.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Measuring $\gamma_{liq}$ — the weight thermometer:** A glass bulb with a capillary is filled with mercury or the liquid. It is weighed empty, filled, then heated. The mass of liquid expelled is measured. $\gamma_{app} = \frac{m_{expelled}}{m_{remaining} \times \Delta T}$.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>**Correction for glass expansion:** Since the glass bulb also expands, the true coefficient is $\gamma_{liq} = \gamma_{app} + \gamma_{glass}$. For mercury in glass: $\gamma_{liq} = 155 \times 10^{-6} + 27 \times 10^{-6} = 182 \times 10^{-6}$ K⁻¹.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>**NEB exam focus:** Distinguish between absolute and apparent expansion. Derive $\gamma_{liq} = \gamma_{app} + \gamma_{glass}$. Explain the anomalous expansion of water and its ecological significance. Solve weight thermometer problems.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>**Ethanol vs. mercury:** Ethanol ($\gamma \approx 750 \times 10^{-6}$ K⁻¹) expands much more than mercury, making ethanol thermometers more sensitive. However, ethanol's boiling point (78°C) limits its range, while mercury works to 357°C.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>**Practical application — liquid-in-glass thermometers:** The scale is calibrated using fixed points (ice point 0°C, steam point 100°C). The apparent expansion determines the distance between divisions. Different liquids give different scales.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>**Common values:** Mercury: $\gamma = 182 \times 10^{-6}$ K⁻¹. Ethanol: $\gamma = 750 \times 10^{-6}$ K⁻¹. Glycerin: $\gamma = 500 \times 10^{-6}$ K⁻¹. Water (above 4°C): $\gamma \approx 210 \times 10^{-6}$ K⁻¹ at 20°C.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>**Why $\gamma_{liq} > \gamma_{solid}$ always:** In liquids, molecules are less tightly bound than in solids. Heating gives molecules more kinetic energy to overcome intermolecular forces, leading to larger volume increases per degree.</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>**Liquid Expansion:** This topic covers the fundamental concepts of liquid expansion in the physics curriculum.</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>Understanding liquid expansion is essential for solving problems and building higher-level mathematical skills.</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>Key definitions and theorems related to liquid expansion should be memorized and practiced regularly.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Key Point 2: Essential theorem or principle related to Liquid expansion: absolute and apparent</td></tr><tr><td>Key Point 3: Important condition or constraint for Liquid expansion: absolute and apparent</td></tr><tr><td>Key Point 4: Common applications of Liquid expansion: absolute and apparent</td></tr><tr><td>Key Point 5: Connection to other topics in Science</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ Apparent expansion equals absolute expansion. ✅ Apparent expansion is LESS than absolute expansion because the container also expands. $\gamma_{app} = \gamma_{liq} - \gamma_{container} < \gamma_{liq}$.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ The apparent expansion of a liquid is independent of the container material. ✅ The apparent expansion DEPENDS on the container. A liquid in a copper container ($\gamma_{Cu} \approx 51 \times 10^{-6}$) shows less apparent expansion than in glass ($\gamma_{glass} \approx 27 \times 10^{-6}$).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Water expands uniformly when cooled from 10°C to 0°C. ✅ Water CONTRACTS when cooled from 10°C to 4°C (reaches maximum density at 4°C), then EXPANDS from 4°C to 0°C. This anomalous behavior is unique among common liquids.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ A liquid's absolute expansion can be measured directly with any container. ✅ It CANNOT — the container always expands. You must either use a container with negligible expansion (impossible) or apply the correction $\gamma_{liq} = \gamma_{app} + \gamma_{container}$.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ The weight of the liquid changes when heated. ✅ The MASS is constant. Only the volume changes. The weight thermometer measures the mass of liquid expelled, not its weight changing.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ All liquids expand when heated. ✅ Most liquids expand, but water between 0°C and 4°C CONTRACTS. Some alloys (like invar) also show anomalous expansion behavior over certain temperature ranges.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Ice floating is due to water's density being the same as liquid water. ✅ Ice is LESS dense than liquid water (about 9% less) because water EXPANDS upon freezing. This is a direct consequence of the anomalous expansion below 4°C.</td></tr><tr><td>Carefully distinguish between similar-looking concepts in liquid expansion.</td></tr><tr><td>Pay attention to the conditions under which formulas and theorems apply.</td></tr><tr><td>Practice with multiple examples to solidify your understanding.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Liquid expansion: absolute and apparent is a fundamental concept in Science that covers the essential principles, formulas, and applications. Understanding this topic is crucial for solving related problems and building foundation for advanced topics. Liquid Expansion is a fundamental concept in Science that covers the essential principles, formulas, and applications. Understanding this topic is crucial for solving related problems and building foundation for advanced topics.</p><hr><h2>Liquid Expansion</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Linear expansion definition:** When a solid is heated, its length increases. The fractional increase in length per unit rise in temperature is called the coefficient of linear expansion, $\alpha$.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Formula:** $\Delta L = L_0 \alpha \Delta T$, where $L_0$ is the original length, $\alpha$ is the coefficient of linear expansion (unit: K⁻¹ or °C⁻¹), and $\Delta T$ is the temperature change.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**New length:** $L = L_0(1 + \alpha\Delta T)$. For small $\alpha\Delta T$, this is an excellent approximation. For large temperature ranges, $\alpha$ may vary and must be treated as a function of temperature.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Physical origin:** Heating increases the amplitude of atomic vibrations in the lattice. Because the interatomic potential is asymmetric (steeper on the compression side), the average separation increases with temperature, causing expansion.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Coefficient of linear expansion for common materials:** Steel: $\alpha \approx 12 \times 10^{-6}$ K⁻¹. Aluminum: $\alpha \approx 24 \times 10^{-6}$ K⁻¹. Copper: $\alpha \approx 17 \times 10^{-6}$ K⁻¹. Invar (Fe-Ni alloy): $\alpha \approx 1.2 \times 10^{-6}$ K⁻¹. Quartz: $\alpha \approx 0.5 \times 10^{-6}$ K⁻¹.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Why metals expand more:** Metals with weaker interatomic bonds (larger bond length, lower melting point) tend to have larger $\alpha$. Lead ($\alpha \approx 29 \times 10^{-6}$) expands more than steel because its atomic bonds are weaker.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Bimetallic strip:** Two metals with different $\alpha$ bonded together bend when heated — the higher-$\alpha$ metal expands more, forcing the strip to curve. Used in thermostats, circuit breakers, and temperature indicators.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Railway tracks — expansion gaps:** Steel rails expand by $\Delta L = L_0 \alpha \Delta T$. For a 12 m rail and $\Delta T = 40°C$: $\Delta L = 12 \times 12 \times 10^{-6} \times 40 = 5.76$ mm. Gaps are left between rails to accommodate this expansion, otherwise buckling occurs.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Measuring $\alpha$ — Searle's apparatus:** A metal rod is heated uniformly. The expansion is measured by a micrometer screw gauge. $\alpha = \frac{\Delta L}{L_0 \Delta T}$. The apparatus minimizes errors from thermal expansion of the frame.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>**Measuring $\alpha$ — Hoke's apparatus:** Uses a glass tube filled with liquid. As the solid rod inside expands, it displaces liquid. The volume of displaced liquid gives $\Delta L$ indirectly. This is an elegant optical-mechanical method.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>**NEB exam focus:** State the formula $\Delta L = L_0 \alpha \Delta T$. Explain the bimetallic strip principle. Describe Searle's apparatus for measuring $\alpha$. Solve numerical problems involving expansion of rods, bridges, and railways.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>**Effect on precision instruments:** Clock pendulums expand with temperature, increasing their period and causing the clock to run slow. $T = 2\pi\sqrt{L/g}$, so $\Delta T/T = \frac{1}{2}\alpha\Delta T$. For steel ($\alpha = 12 \times 10^{-6}$), a 10°C rise causes the clock to lose about 5 seconds per day.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>**Composite rod problem:** Two rods of different materials joined end-to-end: total expansion $\Delta L = L_{01}\alpha_1\Delta T + L_{02}\alpha_2\Delta T$. The junction shifts by the expansion of the first rod.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>**Thermal stress:** If a rod is constrained from expanding, thermal stress develops: $\sigma = Y\alpha\Delta T$, where $Y$ is Young's modulus. For steel ($Y = 200$ GPa, $\alpha = 12 \times 10^{-6}$), a 50°C constraint gives $\sigma = 120$ MPa — significant structural stress.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>**Practical consequence:** Bridges have expansion joints. Glass bottles crack when hot water is poured in because the inner surface expands faster than the outer. Telephone wires sag more in summer than in winter.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ $\Delta L = L_0 \alpha \Delta T$ works for any temperature range. ✅ It is valid only for moderate $\Delta T$ where $\alpha$ is approximately constant. For very large temperature ranges, $\alpha$ varies and the formula becomes approximate.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ All solids expand at the same rate. ✅ Different materials have very different $\alpha$ values. Invar ($\alpha \approx 1.2 \times 10^{-6}$) expands 24 times less than aluminum ($\alpha \approx 24 \times 10^{-6}$).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ The coefficient $\alpha$ has units of temperature. ✅ $\alpha$ has units of K⁻¹ (or °C⁻¹). It is a fractional change per degree, not an absolute change.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ A hole in a metal plate shrinks when heated. ✅ The hole EXPANDS — the material around it expands outward, making the hole larger. This is equivalent to a disk of the same material expanding.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Linear expansion applies to liquids. ✅ Liquids do not have a fixed shape, so linear expansion is meaningless for them. Only cubical (volume) expansion is defined for liquids.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ The expansion gap for railway tracks should be as large as possible. ✅ The gap must be just large enough to prevent buckling. Too large a gap causes excessive noise and wear at the joints.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Thermal stress depends only on $\alpha$ and $\Delta T$. ✅ Thermal stress is $\sigma = Y\alpha\Delta T$ — it also depends on Young's modulus $Y$. A stiffer material develops more stress for the same constraint.</td></tr></table><h2>Dulong and Petit Method for Liquid Expansivity</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Apparatus:** A U-tube made of glass with two vertical limbs and a horizontal base. One limb is kept in ice (0°C) and the other in steam (100°C) or hot water. The liquid in the tube reaches a steady state with different temperatures in each limb.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Principle:** At the horizontal base of the U-tube, the pressure from both limbs must be equal (Pascal's law). The pressure at the base from each limb is $P = \rho g h$, where $\rho$ is the liquid density at that temperature and $h$ is the height of the liquid column.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Equating pressures:** $\rho_0 g h_0 = \rho_{100} g h_{100}$, where $h_0$ is the height in the cold limb (0°C) and $h_{100}$ is the height in the hot limb (100°C). Therefore: $\frac{h_{100}}{h_0} = \frac{\rho_0}{\rho_{100}}$.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Connecting density to expansion:** Since $\rho = \frac{m}{V}$ and mass is constant: $\frac{\rho_0}{\rho_{100}} = \frac{V_{100}}{V_0} = 1 + \gamma_{abs} \Delta T$. Therefore: $\frac{h_{100}}{h_0} = \frac{1}{1 + \gamma_{abs} \Delta T}$.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Solving for $\gamma_{abs}$:** $1 + \gamma_{abs} \Delta T = \frac{h_0}{h_{100}}$. Therefore: $\boxed{\gamma_{abs} = \frac{h_0 - h_{100}}{h_{100} \cdot \Delta T}}$.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Apparent vs absolute:** The heights $h_0$ and $h_{100}$ are read from the glass scale, which itself expands with temperature. The measured heights give $\gamma_{app}$. To get $\gamma_{abs}$: $\gamma_{abs} = \gamma_{app} + \gamma_{glass}$.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Why this method is elegant:** No knowledge of the liquid's mass or volume is needed. Only the difference in heights is measured. The U-tube eliminates errors from container expansion because both limbs are made of the same glass.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Experimental procedure:** (1) Fill the U-tube with the liquid. (2) Place one limb in an ice bath and the other in a steam bath or hot water. (3) Wait for steady state (15–20 minutes). (4) Measure $h_0$ and $h_{100}$ from the scale. (5) Calculate $\gamma_{abs}$.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Source of error — scale expansion:** The glass scale expands with temperature, so the measured heights are slightly smaller than the true heights. The correction is $\gamma_{glass} \approx 2.7 \times 10^{-5}$ K⁻¹ for common glass.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>**Source of error — temperature gradient:** The liquid in each limb may not be at a uniform temperature. The cold limb may warm slightly from the surroundings, and the hot limb may cool. Careful insulation minimizes this.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>**NEB exam focus:** Draw and label the Dulong-Petit apparatus. Derive the formula for $\gamma_{abs}$. Explain the distinction between apparent and absolute expansion. State sources of error and how to minimize them.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>**Sample calculation:** In a U-tube experiment, $h_0 = 50.0$ cm (cold limb at 0°C) and $h_{100} = 51.5$ cm (hot limb at 100°C). $\gamma_{app} = \frac{51.5 - 50.0}{50.0 \times 100} = \frac{1.5}{5000} = 3.0 \times 10^{-4}$ K⁻¹. $\gamma_{abs} = 3.0 \times 10^{-4} + 2.7 \times 10^{-5} = 3.27 \times 10^{-4}$ K⁻¹. This is close to the value for ethanol ($7.5 \times 10^{-4}$) — the liquid may be a different substance or the measurement has error.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>**Comparison with other methods:** The Dulong-Petit method is more accurate than the weight thermometer for liquids with small expansion coefficients. However, it requires a long U-tube and careful temperature control.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>**Historical note:** Pierre Dulong and Alexis Thérèse Petit published this method in 1816. They also discovered the Dulong-Petit law for the molar heat capacity of solids ($C_m \approx 3R \approx 25$ J/(mol·K)), which was one of the first links between thermal and atomic properties.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>**Why the hot limb is taller:** The liquid in the hot limb is less dense, so a taller column is needed to produce the same pressure at the base. This is why $h_{100} > h_0$ — it confirms that heating reduces density.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Key Point 2: Essential theorem or principle related to Dulong and Petit method of determining expansivity of liquid</td></tr><tr><td>Key Point 3: Important condition or constraint for Dulong and Petit method of determining expansivity of liquid</td></tr><tr><td>Key Point 4: Common applications of Dulong and Petit method of determining expansivity of liquid</td></tr><tr><td>Key Point 5: Connection to other topics in Science</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ The formula is $\gamma = \frac{h_0 - h_{100}}{h_0 \Delta T}$. ✅ The correct formula is $\gamma_{abs} = \frac{h_0 - h_{100}}{h_{100} \Delta T}$. The denominator uses $h_{100}$ (the hot limb height), not $h_0$.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ The apparent expansion is greater than the absolute expansion. ✅ Apparent expansion is LESS than absolute expansion because the glass container also expands. $\gamma_{app} = \gamma_{abs} - \gamma_{glass} < \gamma_{abs}$.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ The U-tube method measures absolute expansion directly. ✅ It measures APPARENT expansion (read from the glass scale). The glass expansion must be added to get the absolute expansion.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ The hot limb has a shorter column because the liquid expands. ✅ The hot limb has a TALLER column because the liquid is LESS dense. A longer column of less-dense liquid produces the same pressure as a shorter column of denser liquid.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Both limbs must have the same height for equal pressure. ✅ The pressures are equal, but the heights are DIFFERENT because the densities differ. $\rho_0 h_0 = \rho_{100} h_{100}$, not $h_0 = h_{100}$.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ The method works for any temperature difference. ✅ The method is most accurate when $\Delta T$ is large (0°C to 100°C is ideal). Small $\Delta T$ gives small height differences that are hard to measure accurately.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ The glass tube's expansion affects only the scale, not the liquid columns. ✅ The glass tube expands in all dimensions. The inner diameter increases (affecting volume readings) and the scale markings move apart (affecting height readings). Both effects are small for glass but must be corrected for precision.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Dulong and Petit method of determining expansivity of liquid is a fundamental concept in Science that covers the essential principles, formulas, and applications. Understanding this topic is crucial for solving related problems and building foundation for advanced topics.</p><hr><h2>Dulong and Petit Method of Determining Expansivity of Liquid</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Dulong and Petit's Method: Experimental method to determine absolute coefficient of expansion of a liquid using a U-tube with two limbs at different temperatures. One limb (hot) and one limb (cold) contain the same liquid; columns balance when pressures equal.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Principle: Pressure at same horizontal level in continuous liquid is equal. For two columns of heights h₁ (cold, t₁) and h₂ (hot, t₂) with densities ρ₁, ρ₂: h₁ρ₁g = h₂ρ₂g. Using ρ₂ = ρ₁/(1 + γΔt): derive γ = (h₁ - h₂)/(h₂·t₂ - h₁·t₁).</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Formula: γ = (h₁ - h₂)/(h₂t₂ - h₁t₁) where h₁ = cold column height, h₂ = hot column height, t₁ = cold temperature, t₂ = hot temperature.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Absolute vs Apparent: Dulong-Petit gives absolute expansivity (liquid relative to vacuum) since the glass expansion cancels out in the pressure balance method.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Experimental Setup: U-tube with one limb in ice bath (0°C) and other in steam bath or heated bath. Adjust levels until balance, measure heights and temperatures.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Corrections: Account for vapor pressure of liquid, capillary effects, and non-uniform temperature along columns.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Applications: Used for liquids like water, mercury, alcohol where accurate expansivity needed for thermometer design and thermal engineering.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Dulong-Petit determines absolute liquid expansivity using U-tube at two temperatures.</td></tr><tr><td>Pressure equality: h₁ρ₁ = h₂ρ₂ at same level.</td></tr><tr><td>Formula: γ = (h₁ - h₂)/(h₂t₂ - h₁t₁).</td></tr><tr><td>Method eliminates container expansion effect.</td></tr><tr><td>Requires two temperature baths and height measurements.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Confusing absolute with apparent expansivity: Dulong-Petit gives absolute (no container correction needed).</td></tr><tr><td>Swapping h₁ and h₂ in formula: h₁ is colder/denser/taller column.</td></tr><tr><td>Thinking container expansion matters: It cancels in this method.</td></tr><tr><td>Forgetting temperatures must be different for measurement.</td></tr><tr><td>Using Celsius temperatures directly — fine since ΔT same in °C and K.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Dulong-Petit method determines absolute liquid expansivity using U-tube with columns at two temperatures. Pressure balance h₁ρ₁ = h₂ρ₂ gives γ = (h₁ - h₂)/(h₂t₂ - h₁t₁). Method eliminates container expansion, giving absolute expansivity directly.</p><hr><h2>Dulong and Petit Method</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Dulong–Petit law.** The molar heat capacity of a solid is about 3R ≈ 25 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹ near room temperature — each atom behaves as an independent oscillator with 3 kinetic + 3 potential degrees of freedom.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Finding atomic mass.** Atomic mass ≈ 3R / specific heat; measuring a metal's specific heat therefore gives its equivalent/atomic mass — the classic experiment.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Compounds (Kopp's rule).** For a compound the molecular heat capacity is the sum of the atomic contributions, letting unknown element masses be estimated from measured C.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Limitations.** The law fails at low temperatures — C falls toward zero as T³ (Debye) for metals and much faster for diamond/beryllium; quantum statistics, not classical, explains the falloff.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Exam angle.** Typical questions: state the law with its value, deduce atomic mass from specific heat, or name the solids that obey it least at low temperature.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Carefully distinguish between similar-looking concepts in dulong and petit method.</td></tr><tr><td>Pay attention to the conditions under which formulas and theorems apply.</td></tr><tr><td>Practice with multiple examples to solidify your understanding.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Dulong and Petit Method is a fundamental concept in Science that covers the essential principles, formulas, and applications. Understanding this topic is crucial for solving related problems and building foundation for advanced topics.</p><hr><h2>Comparison Tables</h2><h3>Concept Comparison</h3><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td>1: Linear Expansion and Its Measurement</td><td>**Linear expansion definition:** When a solid is heated, its length increases. The fractional increase in length per unit rise in temperature is called the coefficient of linear expansion, $\alpha$.</td></tr><tr><td>2: Linear Expansion</td><td>Linear Expansion is a fundamental concept in Science that covers the essential principles, formulas, and applications. Understanding this topic is crucial for solving related problems and building foundation for advanced topics.</td></tr><tr><td>3: Cubical and Superficial Expansion</td><td>Cubical expansion, superficial expansion and their relation with linear expansion is a fundamental concept in Science that covers the essential principles, formulas, and applications. Understanding this topic is crucial for solving related problems and building foundation for advanced topics.</td></tr><tr><td>4: Cubical Expansion, Superficial Expansion and Their Relation with Linear Expansion</td><td>Linear ΔL = αL₀ΔT, superficial ΔA = βA₀ΔT (β = 2α), cubical ΔV = γV₀ΔT (γ = 3α) for isotropic solids. α:β:γ = 1:2:3. Liquids have only volume expansion; real = apparent + container. Water shows anomalous expansion below 4°C.</td></tr><tr><td>5: Cubical and Superficial Expansion</td><td>Cubical and Superficial Expansion is a fundamental concept in Science that covers the essential principles, formulas, and applications. Understanding this topic is crucial for solving related problems and building foundation for advanced topics.</td></tr><tr><td>6: Liquid Expansion — Absolute and Apparent</td><td>Liquid expansion: absolute and apparent is a fundamental concept in Science that covers the essential principles, formulas, and applications. Understanding this topic is crucial for solving related problems and building foundation for advanced topics. Liquid Expansion is a fundamental concept in Science that covers the essential principles, formulas, and applications. Understanding this topic is crucial for solving related problems and building foundation for advanced topics.</td></tr><tr><td>7: Liquid Expansion</td><td>**Linear expansion definition:** When a solid is heated, its length increases. The fractional increase in length per unit rise in temperature is called the coefficient of linear expansion, $\alpha$.</td></tr><tr><td>8: Dulong and Petit Method for Liquid Expansivity</td><td>Dulong and Petit method of determining expansivity of liquid is a fundamental concept in Science that covers the essential principles, formulas, and applications. Understanding this topic is crucial for solving related problems and building foundation for advanced topics.</td></tr><tr><td>9: Dulong and Petit Method of Determining Expansivity of Liquid</td><td>Dulong-Petit method determines absolute liquid expansivity using U-tube with columns at two temperatures. Pressure balance h₁ρ₁ = h₂ρ₂ gives γ = (h₁ - h₂)/(h₂t₂ - h₁t₁). Method eliminates container expansion, giving absolute expansivity directly.</td></tr><tr><td>10: Dulong and Petit Method</td><td>Dulong and Petit Method is a fundamental concept in Science that covers the essential principles, formulas, and applications. Understanding this topic is crucial for solving related problems and building foundation for advanced topics.</td></tr></table></body></html>